# `bayesnec`

The `bayesnec` is an R package to fit concentration(dose) — response curves to toxicity data, and derive No-Effect-Concentration (*NEC*), No-Significant-Effect-Concentration (*NSEC*, [@fisherfox2023]), and Effect-Concentration (of specified percentage "x", *ECx*) thresholds from non-linear models fitted using Bayesian Hamiltonian Monte Carlo (HMC) via `brms` [@Burkner2017; @Burkner2018] and `stan`. The package is an adaptation and extension of an initial package `jagsNEC` [@Fisher2020] which was based on the `R2jags` package [@Su2015] and `jags` [@Plummer2003].

# Background

Bayesian model fitting can be difficult to automate across a broad range of usage cases, particularly with respect to specifying valid initial values and appropriate priors. This is one reason the use of Bayesian statistics for *NEC* estimation (or even *ECx* estimation) is not currently widely adopted across the broader ecotoxicological community, who rarely have access to specialist statistical expertise. The `bayesnec` package provides an accessible interface specifically for fitting *NEC* models and other concentration—response models using Bayesian methods. A range of models are specified based on the known distribution of the "concentration" or "dose" variable (the predictor) as well as the "response" variable. The package requires a simplified model formula, which together with the data is used to wrangle more complex non-linear model formula(s), as well as to generate priors and initial values required to call a `brms` model. While the distribution of the predictor and response variables can be specified directly, `bayesnec` will automatically attempt to assign the correct distribution to use based on the characteristics of the provided data.

This project started with an implementation of the *NEC* model based on that described in (Pires et al. 2002) and [@Fox2010] using R2jags [@Fisher2020]. The package has been further generalised to allow a large range of response variables to be modelled using the appropriate statistical distribution. While the original `jagsNEC` implementation supported Gaussian-, Poisson-, Binomial-, Gamma-, Negative Binomial- and Beta-distributed response data, `bayesnec` additionally supports the Beta-Binomial distribution, and can be easily extended to include any of the available `brms` families. We have since also further added a range of alternative *NEC* model types, as well as a range of  concentration—response models (such as 4-parameter logistic and Weibull models) that are commonly used in frequentist-based packages such as `drc`[@Ritz2016]. These models do not employ segmented linear regression (i.e., use of a `step` function) but model the response as a smooth function of concentration. They can be used to derive no-effect toxicity estimates as a no-significant-effect-concentration [@fisherfox2023].

Specific models can be fit directly using `bnec`, which is what we discuss here. Alternatively, it is possible to fit a custom model set, a specific model set, or all the available models. Further information on fitting multi-models using `bayesnec` can be found in the [Multi model usage][e2] vignette. For detailed information on the models available in `bayesnec` see the [Model details][e2b] vignette.

Important information on the current package is contained in the `bayesnec` help-files and the [Model details][e2b] vignette.

This package is currently under development. We are keen to receive any feedback regarding usage, and especially bug reporting that includes an easy to run self-contained reproducible example of unexpected behaviour, or example model fits that fail to converge (have poor chain mixing) or yield other errors. Such information will hopefully help us towards building a more robust package. We cannot help troubleshoot issues if an easy-to-run reproducible example is not supplied.

# Installation

To install the latest release version from CRAN use

```{r eval = FALSE}
install.packages("bayesnec")
```

The current development version can be downloaded from GitHub via

```{r eval = FALSE}
if (!requireNamespace("remotes")) {
  install.packages("remotes")
}
remotes::install_github("open-aims/bayesnec", ref = "dev")
```

Because `bayesnec` is based on `brms` and [Stan](https://mc-stan.org/), a C++
compiler is required. The program
[Rtools](https://cran.r-project.org/bin/windows/Rtools/) comes with a C++
compiler for Windows. On Mac, you should install Xcode. See the prerequisites
section on this
[link](https://github.com/stan-dev/rstan/wiki/RStan-Getting-Started)
for further instructions on how to get the compilers running.

The [Installation and setup][e5] vignette describes this in detail, including
both `brms` back ends (`rstan` and `cmdstanr`), the `cmdstanr` installation used
to build these vignettes, and the failures most often encountered on Windows.

To run this vignette, we will also need some additional packages

```{r, warning = FALSE, message = FALSE}
library(dplyr)
library(ggplot2)
library(tidyr)
```

# The scale of the predictor

Concentration—response experiments are usually designed as a dilution series, in which each concentration is a fixed multiple of the one below it. On a linear scale this design places the low concentrations close together and the high concentrations far apart. The model equation then uses its flexibility at the top of the range, where the response has often already levelled off, and has few distinct concentrations in the region where the response is changing.

The scale on which the predictor is modelled is therefore an important decision, and one that is left to the user: `bnec` uses the concentration as supplied, without transformation. This decision is best made before any model is fitted, because it will generally have more influence on the results than the choice of model equation. For example, for one algal dataset, fitting `nec4param` to `log(dose)` instead of the raw dose reduced the maximum lack of fit in the mean from 0.2115 to 0.0676 (on a response spanning about 0.7), whereas five different equations fitted to `log(dose)` were all within 1.2 `elpd_loo` of one another.

The appropriate scale can be identified from the spacing of the concentrations in the design, not from the response. If the concentrations are close to evenly spaced after a log transformation, the series was designed on a log scale and is usually best modelled on that scale:

```{r}
# the concentrations used in the Beta example below
conc <- c(0.01, 0.3, 1, 3, 10, 30, 100, 300)

# coefficient of variation of the gaps between adjacent concentrations:
# small means evenly spaced on that scale
spacing_cv <- function(x) sd(diff(sort(x))) / mean(diff(sort(x)))
round(c(linear = spacing_cv(conc), log = spacing_cv(log(conc))), 2)
```

For these concentrations, the coefficient of variation of the gaps is 1.72 on the linear scale and 0.58 on the log scale.

Any simple transformation can be written directly inside `crf` (e.g. `crf(log(x), model = "nec4param")`), and this has two consequences. First, the transformation is evaluated before `bayesnec` checks the predictor, so a control concentration recorded as `0` is passed to the data check as `log(0)`, and `bnec` stops with an error that the predictor contains values that are not finite. `bayesnec` does not correct a zero on the predictor axis on either the recorded or the transformed scale, because a concentration of zero is an ordinary control and no response distribution constrains the values a predictor can take. Where a logarithm is used, an offset therefore needs to be chosen by the user and added to the data before fitting.

Second, the model is fitted on the transformed scale. However, `nec`, `ecx`, `nsec` and the plotting methods return the toxicity estimates on the recorded scale. A function passed as `xform` is instead applied to an estimate on the transformed scale, so `xform = identity` returns the transformed scale and the inverse of the transformation returns the recorded scale, as in the following example:

```{r, eval = FALSE}
ecx(exp_2, xform = function(x) exp(x) - 1)
autoplot(exp_2, xform = function(x) exp(x) - 1)
```

The inline form and a pre-computed column (as in the code below) are equivalent ways of writing the model, and give identical estimates on the transformed scale, whether the transformation is of a bare variable (e.g. `log(x)`) or its argument contains arithmetic (e.g. `log(x + 1)`).

The inline and column forms differ only in the scale on which estimates are returned by default. Because `bayesnec` cannot see the transformation used to create a column, estimates from the column form are on the scale of `log_x` and need `xform = function(x) exp(x) - 1` to return them to the recorded scale, whereas the inline form applies this inverse automatically:

```{r, eval = FALSE}
prop_data$log_x <- log(prop_data$raw_x + 1)
bnec(resp ~ crf(log_x, model = "nec4param"), data = prop_data)
```

# Examples

## Fitting the `nec4param` model using `bnec`

Here we include some examples showing how to use the package to fit an *NEC* model to binomial, proportional, count and continuous response data. The examples are those used at: <https://github.com/gerard-ricardo/NECs/blob/master/NECs>, but here we are showing how to fit models to these data using the `bayesnec` package. Note however, the default behaviour in `bayesnec` is to use the `"identity"` link because the native link functions for each family (e.g., `"logit"` for Binomial, `"log"` for Poisson) introduce non-linear transformation on formulas which are already non-linear. So please be aware that estimates of *NEC* or *ECx* might not be as expected when using link functions other than identity (see the [Model details][e2b] vignette for more information and the models available in `bayesnec`).

### Binomial data

The response variable is considered to follow a binomial distribution when it is a count out of a total (such as the percentage survival of individuals, for example). First, we read in the binomial example from [pastebin](https://pastebin.com/tools), prepare the data for analysis, and then inspect the dataset as well as the "concentration", in this case `raw_x`.


```{r}
binom_data <- "https://pastebin.com/raw/zfrUha88" |>
  read.table(header = TRUE, dec = ",", stringsAsFactors = FALSE) |>
  dplyr::rename(raw_x = raw.x) |>
  dplyr::mutate(raw_x = as.numeric(as.character(raw_x)))

str(binom_data)
summary(binom_data$raw_x)
```

In this case for `raw_x`, lowest concentration is `r min(binom_data$raw_x)` and the highest is `r max(binom_data$raw_x)`. The data are right skewed and on the continuous scale. This type of distribution for the predictor data are common for concentration—response experiments, where the *concentration* data are the concentration of contaminants, or dilutions. The current default in `bayesnec` is to estimate the appropriate distribution(s) and priors based on the data, but it is possible to supply `prior` or `family` directly as additional arguments to the underlying `brm` function from `brms`. We are going to model this with the predictor data on a log scale, as this is the scaling clearly used in the experimental design and thus will provide more stable results.

The data are binomial, with the column `suc` indicating the number of successes in the binomial call, and column `tot` indicating the number of trials.

The main working function in `bayesnec` is the function `bnec`, which calls the other necessary functions and fits the `brms` model. We can run `bnec` by supplying `formula`: a special formula which comprises the relationship between response and predictor, and the C-R model (or models) chosen to be fitted; and `data`: a `data.frame` containing the data for the model fitting, here, `binom_data`.

The basic `bayesnec` formula should be of the form:

```{r, eval = FALSE}
suc | trials(tot) ~ crf(log(raw_x), model = "your_model")
```

where the left-hand side of the formula is implemented exactly as in `brms`, including the `trials` term. The right-hand side of the formula contains the special internal function `crf` (which stands for concentration-response function), and takes the predictor (including any simple function transformations such as `log`) and the desired C-R model or suite of models. As with any formula in R, the name of the terms need to be exactly as they are in the input data.frame.

`bnec` will guess the data types for use, although as mentioned above we could manually specify `family` as `"binomial"` (or `binomial`, or `binomial()`). `bnec` will also generate appropriate priors for the `brms` model call, although these can also be specified manually (see the [Priors][e3] vignette for more details).

```{r, warning = FALSE, message = FALSE, results = "hide"}
library(bayesnec)

exp_1 <- bnec(suc | trials(tot) ~ crf(log(raw_x), model = "nec4param"),
              data = binom_data, seed = 333)
```

The function shows the progress of the `brms` fit and returns the usual `brms` output (with a few other elements added to this list). The function `plot(pull_brmsfit(exp_1))` can be used to plot the chains, so we can assess mixing and look for other potential issues with the model fit. We can also run a pairs plot that can help to assess issues with identifiability, and which also looks OK. There are a range of other model diagnostics that can be explored for `brms` model fits, using the function `pull_brmsfit`. We encourage you to explore the rich material already on GitHub regarding use and validation of [brms](https://github.com/paul-buerkner/brms) models.

Initially `bayesnec` will attempt to use starting values generated for that type of model formula and family. It will run the iterations and then test if all chains are valid. If the model does not have valid chains `bayesnec` will discard that model and an error will be returned indicating the model could not be fit successfully.

Whenever the `model` argument of `crf` is a single model `bnec` will return an object of class `bayesnecfit`. We can see the summary of our fitted model parameters using:

```{r}
summary(exp_1)
```

Note the Rhat values in this example are one, indicating convergence.

The functions `plot` (extending `base` plot method) and `autoplot` (extending `ggplot2` plot method) can be used to plot the fitted model. You can also make your own plot from the data included in the returned object (class `bayesnecfit`) from the call to `bnec`. **Notice, however, that in this case we log-transformed the predictor `raw_x` in the input formula.** This causes `brms` to pass these transformations onto Stan's native code, so the parameters of the model (including `nec`) are estimated on the log scale, although the derived toxicity estimates and the plots are returned on the data scale. The additional argument `xform` is applied to an estimate on the fitted scale, so supplying the inverse of the transformation (`exp`, as in the call below) also returns the data scale. Because the concentrations from 6.25 to 400 are a two-fold dilution series, we also plot them on a log axis (`scale_x_continuous(transform = "log10")`), on which these steps are evenly spaced (the lowest concentration, 0.1, is about six steps below the next).

```{r exmp1-binom, fig.width = 5, message = FALSE}
autoplot(exp_1, xform = exp) +
  scale_x_continuous(transform = "log10")
```

There are many built in methods available for `brmsfit` objects and we encourage you to make use of these in full.

This model fit does not look great. You can see that the error bounds around the fit are far too narrow for this data given the variability among the points, suggesting over dispersion of this model (meaning that the data are more variable than this model fit predicts). For models whose response data are Binomial- or Poisson-distributed, an estimate of dispersion is provided by `bayesnec`, and this can be extracted using `exp_1$dispersion`. Values > 1 indicate overdispersion and values < 1 indicate underdispersion. In this case the overdispersion value is much bigger than 1, suggesting extreme overdispersion (meaning our model does not properly capture the true variability represented in this data). We would need to consider alternative ways of modelling this data using a different distribution, such as the Beta-Binomial.

```{r}
exp_1$dispersion
```

### Beta-Binomial

The Beta-Binomial model can be useful for overdispersed Binomial data.

```{r, results = "hide"}
exp_1b <- bnec(suc | trials(tot) ~ crf(log(raw_x), model = "nec4param"),
               data = binom_data, family = beta_binomial(link = "identity"),
               seed = 333)
```

Fitting this data with the `beta_binomial` yields a much more realistic fit in terms of the confidence bounds (check `autoplot(exp_1b, xform = exp)`) and the spread in the data. Note that a dispersion estimate is not provided here, as overdispersion is only relevant for Poisson and Binomial data.


```{r exmp1-bbinom, fig.width = 5, message = FALSE}
autoplot(exp_1b, xform = exp) +
  scale_x_continuous(transform = "log10")
```

```{r message = FALSE}
exp_1b$dispersion
```

Now that we have a better fit to these data, we can interpret the results. The estimated *NEC* value can be obtained directly from the fitted model object. The posterior draws stored in the object are on the fitted scale, so here they are transformed back to the data scale (`nec(exp_1b)` returns this estimate on the data scale directly).
 
```{r}
summary(exp(exp_1b$ne_posterior))
```

EC*x* estimates can also be obtained from the *NEC* model fit, using the function `ecx`. Note these may differ from a typical 4-parameter non-linear model, as the *NEC* model is a broken stick non-linear regression and will often fall more sharply than a smooth 4-parameter non-linear curve. See both the [Model details][e2b] and [Comparing posterior predictions][e4] vignettes for more information.

```{r, warning = FALSE}
ecx(exp_1b, xform = exp)
```

A summary method has also been developed for `bayesnecfit` objects that gives an overall summary of the model statistics. This summary includes the estimate of *NEC* in two places: the `nec` population-level effect, which is on the scale on which the predictor was fitted (here `log(raw_x)`), and the *NEC* row, which is on the data scale.

```{r, warning = FALSE}
summary(exp_1b)
```

### Beta data

Sometimes the response variable is distributed between `0` and `1` but is not a straight-forward Binomial because it is a proportion on the continuous scale. A common example in coral ecology is maximum quantum yield (the proportion of light used for photosynthesis when all reaction centres are open) which is a measure of photosynthetic efficiency calculated from Pulse-Amplitude-Modulation (PAM) data. Here we have a proportion value that is not based on trials and successes. In this case there are no theoretical trials, and the data must be modelled using a Beta distribution instead.

These data are from a three-fold dilution series, so (as described in the section on the scale of the predictor above) we model the predictor on a log scale. In this case we use `log(raw_x + 1)` instead of `log(raw_x)`, because the lowest concentration (0.01) is thirty times below the next, whereas each of the other steps is a factor of three. On a plain log scale this point would be 3.4 log units from its neighbour, compared with about 1.1 for each of the other gaps, which would give a single concentration a great deal of leverage over the fitted curve. Adding the offset reduces the distance between this point and its neighbour, and the series is then more evenly spaced than under `log(raw_x)`:

```{r}
round(c(linear = spacing_cv(conc),
        log = spacing_cv(log(conc)),
        `log(x + 1)` = spacing_cv(log(conc + 1))), 2)
```

With the offset, the coefficient of variation of the gaps is 0.44, against 0.58 for `log(raw_x)`. An offset of one is chosen for convenience and not on any principle, although it is reasonable here because the concentrations span several orders of magnitude and the value one is within their range. Because the offset is written inside `crf()`, it is part of the transformation that the estimators and the plots invert, and these therefore return concentrations. The `xform` passed below is this inverse written out explicitly, and gives an identical figure. The concentration axis is drawn with `scale_x_continuous(transform = "log1p")`, which is the scale on which the model was fitted.

```{r, results = "hide"}
prop_data <- "https://pastebin.com/raw/123jq46d" |>
  read.table(header = TRUE, dec = ",", stringsAsFactors = FALSE) |>
  dplyr::rename(raw_x = raw.x) |>
  dplyr::mutate(across(where(is.character), as.numeric))

exp_2 <- bnec(resp ~ crf(log(raw_x + 1), model = "nec4param"),
              data = prop_data, seed = 333)
```

```{r exmp1-beta, warning = FALSE, message = FALSE, fig.width = 5}
autoplot(exp_2, xform = function(x)exp(x) - 1) +
  scale_x_continuous(transform = "log1p", breaks = c(0, 1, 3, 10, 30, 100, 300))
```

### Poisson data

Where data are a count (of, for example, individuals or cells), the response is likely to be Poisson distributed. Such data are distributed from `0` to `Inf` and are integers. Here we simulate counts with a mean that follows a `nec4param` curve across the concentrations used elsewhere in this vignette, and then summarise the concentration (i.e. predictor) data.

```{r}
set.seed(333)
data(nec_data)
# The counts are drawn from a Poisson whose mean follows the curve, rather than
# built by adding Gaussian noise to a proportion and rounding. Additive noise of
# constant standard deviation leaves a variance that does not change with the
# mean, and no count distribution can represent that: neither a Poisson nor a
# Negative binomial fit would be adequate, so such data cannot be used to
# illustrate the difference between them.
nec4 <- function(x, top, bot, nec, beta) {
  bot + (top - bot) * exp(-exp(beta) * (x - nec) * (x > nec))
}
count_mu <- nec4(nec_data$x, top = 85, bot = 5, nec = 1.5, beta = 0.3)
count_data <- data.frame(
  x = nec_data$x,
  y = as.integer(rpois(length(count_mu), count_mu))
)

str(count_data)
summary(count_data$y)
summary(count_data$x)
```

First, we supply `bnec` with `data` (here `count_data`), and specify our formula. As we have integers of 0 and greater, the `family` is `"poisson"`. The default behaviour to guess the variable types works for this example.

```{r, warning = FALSE, results = "hide"}
exp_3 <- bnec(y ~ crf(x, model = "nec4param"), data = count_data,
              seed = 333)
```

```{r exmp1-poisson, warning = FALSE, fig.width = 5}
autoplot(exp_3)
exp_3$dispersion
```

The dispersion estimate (described in the Binomial example above) is close to one (0.96), as it should be for data simulated from a Poisson distribution, and the width of the credible bands is consistent with the spread of the points. We would also plot the model chains (with `plot(exp_3$fit)`) and the parameter estimates to check the fit.

### Negative binomial

Count data are often more variable than a Poisson distribution allows (a situation known as *overdispersion*), and in that case the Negative binomial distribution is generally used. To illustrate the difference, we simulate a second set of counts from the curve used above, but with additional (extra-Poisson) variability.

```{r}
set.seed(333)
count_data_od <- data.frame(
  x = nec_data$x,
  y = as.integer(rnbinom(length(count_mu), mu = count_mu, size = 20))
)
```

Fitting these data with a Poisson distribution gives credible bands that are far too narrow for the variability among the points and a dispersion estimate well above one, which indicates that a different distribution should be used.

```{r, warning = FALSE, results = "hide"}
exp_3b <- bnec(y ~ crf(x, model = "nec4param"), data = count_data_od,
               seed = 333)
```

```{r exmp1-poisson-od, warning = FALSE, fig.width = 5}
autoplot(exp_3b)
exp_3b$dispersion
```

We can refit these data by deliberately specifying the Negative binomial distribution via the `family` argument.

```{r, warning = FALSE, results = "hide"}
exp_3c <- bnec(y ~ crf(x, model = "nec4param"), data = count_data_od,
               family = "negbinomial", seed = 333)
```

The resultant plot has wider and more realistic credible bands than the Poisson fit to these data. Note that a dispersion estimate is not returned here, because `bayesnec` calculates one only for the Poisson and Binomial families. See the [Model details][e2b] and [Multi model usage][e2] vignettes for options to fit other models to these data.

```{r exmp1-negbinbase, warning = FALSE, fig.width = 5}
autoplot(exp_3c)
```

### Counts observed over an exposure

A count is very often observed over an exposure that differs among replicates (e.g. offspring counted in replicates with different numbers of fecund adults, cells counted over different areas, or events counted over different lengths of time). The `rate` term is used to declare this exposure, and is available for the `poisson` and `negbinomial` families. Here we simulate counts of offspring where the number of females varies among replicates.

```{r}
set.seed(333)
n_females <- sample(8:24, nrow(nec_data), replace = TRUE)
fecundity_data <- data.frame(
  x = nec_data$x,
  n_females = n_females,
  # The curve gives a rate -- offspring per female -- and the Poisson mean is
  # that rate multiplied by the number of females in the replicate, so the
  # response is a genuine count rather than a ratio.
  y = as.integer(rpois(nrow(nec_data),
                       nec4(nec_data$x, top = 6, bot = 0.4, nec = 1.5,
                            beta = 0.3) * n_females))
)
```

The raw counts are passed to `bnec` unchanged, so a replicate with twenty females contributes more information to the fit than one with eight. This difference in information would be lost if `y / n_females` were fitted as a continuous response.

```{r, warning = FALSE, results = "hide"}
exp_3d <- bnec(y | rate(n_females) ~ crf(x, model = "nec4param"),
               data = fecundity_data, family = "poisson",
               seed = 333)
```

Because `bnec` uses an identity link, `brms` applies the denominator multiplicatively on the response scale, and not as a log offset on the linear predictor. The fitted mean is therefore the rate itself, so `top` and `bot` can be interpreted directly as offspring per female (and `nec` as a concentration, as in the other examples). The denominator is set to 1 on the prediction grid, so the fitted curve (and any `ecx` or `nsec` estimate derived from it) is on this scale, and `autoplot` divides the observed counts by the denominator so that they are plotted on this scale as well.

```{r exmp1-rate, warning = FALSE, fig.width = 5}
autoplot(exp_3d)
```

```{r}
exp_3d$ne
ecx(exp_3d, ecx_val = 10)
exp_3d$dispersion
```

The estimated *NEC* of 1.53 (95% credible interval 1.45 to 1.61) includes the value of 1.5 used to simulate these data, and the dispersion estimate (0.88) is close to one, as expected for counts simulated from a Poisson distribution.

The interpretation of a `rate` fit does, however, depend on why the exposure varies. Where it varies for reasons unrelated to the treatment (e.g. a replicate simply received fewer animals), `rate` gives the intended estimate. Where the exposure is itself an effect of the treatment (e.g. if `n_females` were the number of females *surviving* to the time of counting), the endpoint has silently changed from reproductive output to fecundity conditional on survival. Both are legitimate, but they are different endpoints and will not return the same *NEC*.

### Measure data

Where data are a measured variable, the response is likely to be Gamma distributed. Good examples of `Gamma`-distributed data include measures of body size, such as length, weight, or area. Such data are distributed from `0+` to `Inf` and are continuous. Here we use the `nec_data` supplied with `bayesnec` with the response `y` on the exponential scale to ensure the right data range for a Gamma as an example.

```{r}
data(nec_data)
measure_data <- nec_data |>
  dplyr::mutate(measure = exp(y))
```

```{r, warning = FALSE, results = "hide"}
exp_4 <- bnec(measure ~ crf(x, model = "nec4param"), data = measure_data,
              seed = 333)
```

```{r exmp1-gammabase, warning = FALSE, fig.width = 5}
autoplot(exp_4)
summary(exp_4)
```

In this case our model has converged really well.

# Preparing the response

Normalising the response to the control is conventional in ecotoxicology, usually as $y^0_i = 1 - y_i / \bar{y}_{con}$, where $\bar{y}_{con}$ is the mean of three to six control replicates. @Ritz2026 quantified the consequences of this practice. Because all observations are divided by a single *random* quantity, the normalised values are correlated, and the sampling variability of the divisor is discarded. In addition, applying Jensen's inequality to $y \rightarrow 1/y$ gives $E(1 - y_i/\bar{y}_{con}) \leq 1 - E(y_i)/E(\bar{y}_{con})$, so the inhibition trend is biased downwards and the effective doses estimated from it are biased upwards. In their simulation of an ED10 with six controls, normalising gave a bias of 6.8% and a coefficient of variation of 26.4%, compared with 2.1% and 12.7% for the ED10 estimated from the raw response, and the coverage of nominal 95% intervals was 88–91%. Roughly half the reported variability was an artefact of the normalisation itself.

For these reasons, supply the response to `bnec` as measured, and do not convert it to percent inhibition, percent of control, or percent of the observed maximum beforehand. Nothing is lost by not normalising, because the concentration at which inhibition increases by $x$ percent is the concentration at which the response declines by $x$ percent, so both analyses answer the same question. This concentration is what `ecx(type = "absolute")` (the default) already returns:

$$f(EC_x, \beta) = (1 - x/100) \cdot f(0, \beta)$$

where the decline is relative to the *fitted* control value, not to an observed control mean. Because this is evaluated separately within each posterior draw, the uncertainty in the control level is propagated into the credible interval instead of being discarded. This is the estimand @Ritz2026 recommend, and following their advice therefore requires no change to `bayesnec`, only a change to the data supplied to it.

Dividing by the maximum observed response, instead of the control mean, is worse for three reasons. First, an extreme order statistic is more variable and more biased than a mean of three to six values. Second, the divisor then depends on all treatments and not only on the controls, so the treated groups partly determine their own denominator. Third, it forces a single observation to equal `1`, which is outside the open $(0, 1)$ support of the Beta distribution. `bnec` must then shift this observation off the boundary, which is a further distortion in addition to the first two.

`bnec` checks for both practices and gives a message where it detects either. Dividing by the observed maximum gives a maximum of exactly `1` that is attained by only one observation, and dividing by the control mean gives control observations with a mean of exactly `1`. Each message cites @Ritz2026 and `?ecx`. The first check is not applied when the response is on a rational grid (i.e. all values are multiples of $1/n$ for a single integer $n$ up to 100), because genuine proportions derived from counts (e.g. 19/20 surviving) routinely include a single observation at `1` and are not evidence of normalisation.

## Unavoidable divisors

The problem is division by a *random* quantity, not division as such, and @Ritz2026 are explicit on this point:

> If the average growth rate in the control group was known beyond uncertainty, perhaps from historical data, there would be no problem.

This distinction is important for the `Beta` and `zero_inflated_beta` families, which require a response on the open interval $(0, 1)$ and therefore sometimes need a divisor. Where a divisor is required, it must be a constant fixed before the analysis (a physiological or design ceiling, or a value from accumulated historical controls), and never a quantity calculated from the dataset being analysed.

Note also that ECx is invariant to the choice of a constant divisor, because it is a relative decline from the fitted `top`. Changing the divisor changes the meaning of `top`, but not the toxicity estimate.

Many proportional responses do not need a divisor at all. For example, the maximum quantum yield used in the Beta example above is a genuine physical proportion on $(0, 1)$, as are a survival fraction and a bleached-area fraction. These responses are already on the appropriate scale, and should be modelled as they are.

# Censoring

A recorded value is *censored* when the true value is known to be within an interval, and not at a point (e.g. a concentration below a limit of detection, a cell count below the resolution of the method, or a value rounded at the recording precision). The number recorded is therefore a *bound*, not a measurement. Substituting a value for it (such as zero, half the detection limit, or the bound itself treated as though it had been observed) implies more than is known. `bayesnec` passes through the `cens()` addition term (aterm) from `brms`, so that the bound can be declared instead:

```r
y | cens(censoring) ~ crf(x, model = "nec4param")
```

The `censoring` term is a variable in the data and not a constant, because in a concentration-response dataset only some rows are censored. It takes the values `"none"`, `"left"`, `"right"` or `"interval"` (equivalently `0`, `-1`, `1`, `2`), and interval censoring takes a second argument giving the upper bound, `cens(censoring, upper)`.

For a censored row, the value in the response column is the bound. On a left-censored row this is the value at or below which the true value is known to be, and not zero or a guess at the unknown true value. The declaration supplies the upper end of the interval and the support of the family supplies the lower end, so a left-censored row under a Beta distribution implies only that the true value is in $(0, \text{bound}]$.

Because the bound has been declared, a censored row is exempt from the boundary shifts that `bnec` otherwise applies to zeros under the Gamma and Beta families (and to ones under the Beta family), as shifting the bound would change what was declared. However, declaring a row censored at a value the family excludes (left-censored at `0` under a Gamma or Beta distribution, or right-censored at `1` under a Beta distribution) gives an error and not a shift, because the censored likelihood is degenerate there.

The reason for declaring the bound is the contribution each row makes to the likelihood. A censored row contributes $F(\text{bound})$ (the probability of a value at or below the bound), and not a density at a point. As the fitted curve descends past the bound, $F$ increases towards 1 and then *saturates*, so the curve is not rewarded for passing further below the bound, but it is not penalised either. In contrast, a substituted value contributes a density, which declines as the curve descends, so substitution actively pulls the curve back up towards the substituted value.

## Limits of the censored likelihood

This saturation prevents the censored likelihood from implying more than the data show, although it also limits what the data can identify. Once the fitted curve is a few residual standard deviations below the bound, $F(\text{bound})$ is indistinguishable from 1, and lowering the curve further does not change the likelihood at all. The likelihood is *flat* over that region, so a parameter that is expressed only in that region is not identified by the data, and its posterior is determined by the prior.

The lower asymptote is the parameter most often affected. Where every response near the bottom of the curve is censored, the data indicate only that `bot` is somewhere below the bound, and nothing in the likelihood distinguishes one such value from another.

As an example, we use the `alga` dataset supplied with the package, for *Rhodomonas salina* under contaminant A (the combination in which the population fell below the counting limit at the highest concentrations, so that specific growth rate is undefined there). These 16 rows are flagged `sgr_source == "substituted"`. A counting limit of 10 cells mL^-1^, against the recorded `density_initial` of 3871 cells mL^-1^ over three days, implies a bound of $(\log 10 - \log 3871)/3 = -1.99$ d^-1^. We fitted `nec4param` with a Gaussian family and the same prior throughout, and varied only how these rows were declared:

| below-limit rows declared as | `bot`, median [95% CI] | posterior SD |
|---|---|---|
| the bound, treated as observed | −1.985 [−2.013, −1.958] | 0.014 |
| `cens(censoring)`, left-censored at the bound | −5.64 [−11.0, −2.51] | 2.23 |
| `cens(censoring, upper)`, interval-censored from extinction to the bound | −2.45 [−2.68, −2.10] | 0.17 |

When the rows are substituted (first row of the table), the estimate is not only wrong about the value but also overconfident, with a posterior SD of 0.014 and an interval a tenth the width of either of the others. This is because these rows have been treated as being exactly at the bound, when all that is known is that they are below it.

Declaring the rows left-censored removes this false assumption, and shows that the data contain almost no information about `bot`. The posterior SD increases by a factor of 160, and the interval extends down to −11 d^-1^, a rate at which the population would decline by a factor of $e^{33}$ over the test. This is the lower tail of the prior, not an estimate. There is no mechanical problem with this fit, and nothing that a routine check would stop on: the chains mix, $\widehat{R}$ is 1.00, and the sampler gave 5 divergent transitions in 8000 draws. The estimate is simply uninformed by the data, and an interval alone does not show this.

Interval censoring (third row of the table) resolves this problem. A population below the counting limit is not below extinction, so the true value is within an interval, and `cens(censoring, upper)` declares this interval. Bounding the flat region from below restores a usable posterior. However, the lower end of the interval is then the declared extinction floor (here 1 cell mL^-1^, and therefore −2.75 d^-1^), and the lower end of the posterior interval is close to it (−2.68, against −2.75). The asymptote is constrained by this assumption and not measured, and should be reported as such. This fit also needed a longer warmup and a smaller step size to converge (with the default settings, one chain of four became stranded on a ridge in `beta`, with $\widehat{R} = 1.47$), because the interval declaration gives the curve freedom in the region that the other two treatments fix, and this freedom has to be sampled.

There are two implications for practice. First, identification should be judged from the contraction from prior to posterior, and not from the interval. Across these three fits the contraction was 0.996, 0.379 and 0.953 respectively, and the middle value identifies the problem that the interval does not show. This can be computed from `pull_prior()` and the posterior draws. Second, the problem propagates, because any quantity derived from the deep part of the fitted curve (well below the bound) also depends on the prior, so an `ecx()` value whose target is near the asymptote should be treated with as much caution as `bot` itself.

Whether a value is censored at all is a separate question from how to handle it once it is. A zero that marks a distinct event (e.g. an organism that died, and not a measurement that fell below a limit) is structural, and should be modelled with a hurdle model and not with a censoring declaration (see `vignette("example6")`).

# Non-constant dispersion

By default, the dispersion parameter of the chosen family is held constant across the whole concentration—response curve. For a `gaussian` response this assumes that replicate variability is constant across concentrations, and for the other families it means that the dispersion parameter (and hence the coefficient of variation or precision) does not change with the mean. Where this assumption is wrong, the credible intervals of the toxicity estimates are affected unevenly, because the assumed variability is too large at one end of the concentration series and too small at the other.

The special term `disp` relaxes this assumption, in one of two ways:

```r
y ~ crf(x, model = "nec4param") + disp(~x)         # on the predictor
y ~ crf(x, model = "nec4param") + disp("power")    # on the fitted mean
```

The first form, `disp(~x)`, is an ordinary `brms` distributional formula, so any formula that `brms` accepts can be used (including `disp(~log(x))` and `disp(~s(x))`). This form specifies only that variability differs along the concentration axis. The second form, `disp("power")`, instead models the dispersion parameter as a function of the *fitted mean*, which is a statement about the measurement process and not about the concentration axis. These forms coincide for a monotone curve, where the fitted mean is itself a monotone function of the predictor, but differ under hormesis or where a design revisits a fitted mean (i.e. one fitted mean occurs at more than one concentration). The available variance functions, the families each applies to, and what the estimated exponent implies for a given family are described in `?bayesnecformula`.

A zero concentration restricts what can be written inside `disp(~x)`. The formula is passed to `brms` unchanged, although `bnec` first evaluates each of its terms on the data supplied, so `disp(~log(x))` with a zero concentration stops with an error naming the term `log(x)`. As for a transformation inside `crf` (see the section on the scale of the predictor), the remedy is to add an offset to the data and use that column in the formula.

A variance function of the fitted mean requires the mean to be modelled on its natural scale. This is because the sub-model is built by substituting the model's own curve expression into the variance function, and that expression is the linear predictor on the *link* scale. `bnec` fits on the identity link, and assigns it unless a link argument is written, so no action is needed in the usual case (naming the family, as in `family = "Gamma"` or `family = Gamma()`, is enough). Other links are refused instead of fitted, because under an inverse link the sub-model would estimate the slope with the opposite sign, while still converging without difficulty.

## Choosing a variance function

The `"power"` and `"twosided"` variance functions are written in terms of $\log(\mu)$, and are therefore undefined wherever the fitted mean is zero or negative. This rules them out for a response on the real line (e.g. a specific growth rate, a yield or an increment, all of which can be negative). Where such a response is fitted with a `gaussian` family and includes values at or below zero, `bnec` refuses either function before fitting, so that the fit does not fail at initialisation. The error message suggests `"loglinear"` or `disp(~x)` instead.

The `"loglinear"` function is linear in $\mu$ and not in $\log(\mu)$, so it remains defined across zero. It is also the form that a log-transformed endpoint inherits from a power law on its original scale. If a density has $\text{sd} \propto \mu^p$, the delta method gives $\text{sd}(\log N) \propto \mu^{p - 1}$, and substituting $\mu_N = N_0 \exp(d \mu_r)$ for an average specific growth rate $\mu_r$ over $d$ days gives $\log \text{sd}(\mu_r) = \text{const} + d (p - 1) \mu_r$, which is the `"loglinear"` form. A growth rate can therefore be described by a variance function (although not by the power law), and $p < 1$ implies $c_1 < 0$.

The exponent $c_1$ is dimensionless for `"power"` and `"twosided"`, which multiply $\log(\mu)$, but has units of 1/response for `"loglinear"`. Its default prior is therefore scaled to the observed spread of the response, so that its interpretation does not depend on the units in which the response is measured.

The covariate in each variance function is centred on a fixed reference value $m$, which is computed from the response before fitting (its median, or its geometric median for the forms that take $\log(\mu)$). Because $m$ is a constant and not an estimated quantity, it changes the coordinates in which the sub-model is written, but not the likelihood. Without centring, $c_0$ would be the dispersion parameter at $\mu = 1$ (or at $\mu = 0$ for `"loglinear"`), which is far from the data unless the response happens to be of order one. For example, fitting a response of order $10^4$ without centring gives a posterior correlation between $c_0$ and $c_1$ above 0.99. With centring, $c_0$ is the dispersion parameter at a typical response, and $c_1$ is its rate of change from there.

## Diagnosis before modelling

Replicate variability that appears to change across a concentration series usually has some other cause, and a variance function will absorb any of these causes and still give a confident estimate of the slope. We would therefore encourage treating `disp` as a diagnostic, and not as a routine addition to a model, and suggest excluding three explanations before concluding that dispersion is genuinely non-constant.

The first is substituted or censored values (the subject of the Censoring section above). Values floored at a boundary, or substituted for a detection limit, cluster at one end of the series, and their spread is easily mistaken for heteroscedasticity. Refitting the model with those rows declared censored is the test for this.

The second is a misspecified family. A variance function fitted under one family may recover only what a different family assumes without any additional parameters. For example, a constant coefficient of variation is what a `Gamma` already implies, so fitting `disp("power")` to a constant-CV response under a `gaussian` family will appear to be a large improvement, while changing nothing that a `Gamma` would not have handled. Comparing the baseline fits across candidate families, before adding any dispersion term, distinguishes between these explanations.

The third is lack of fit in the mean. Residuals from a curve that is systematically wrong in some region are large in that region, and a variance function can describe the region as noisy instead of the curve as wrong. The check for this is to express the departure of the fitted curve from the observed mean at each concentration in units of the *within-concentration replicate standard deviation*, and not in units of the response.

## A worked example

We simulate a response with a standard deviation that increases with the mean, so that the relationship is known and not assumed, and confirm that it is recovered. The response here is strictly positive, so `bnec` will select a `Gamma` family, which already implies a standard deviation proportional to the mean. We therefore simulate a standard deviation proportional to $\mu^{0.4}$, which is shallower than the family itself provides, so that the coefficient of variation increases as the response declines and there is a relationship for a variance function to estimate. A relationship steeper than that of the family would be outside the range admitted by the default prior on the exponent (see `?bayesnecformula`). Choosing the simulated relationship with the family in mind applies the second check above (a misspecified family) when designing the example, instead of after fitting.

```{r disp-sim}
set.seed(333)
disp_x <- rep(c(0.1, 0.3, 1, 3, 10, 30, 100), each = 12)
disp_mu <- 2 + (18 - 2) / (1 + exp((log(disp_x) - log(6)) * exp(0.5)))
disp_dat <- data.frame(
  x = disp_x,
  y = rnorm(length(disp_mu), disp_mu, 0.2 * disp_mu ^ 0.4)
)
```

The relationship can be seen without fitting any model, by summarising the replicate variability within each concentration. Because these summaries are calculated within concentrations, no curve is involved in the calculation, and lack of fit in the mean cannot inflate them.

```{r disp-within}
within_conc <- aggregate(y ~ x, disp_dat, function(z) c(mean = mean(z), sd = sd(z)))
data.frame(
  x = within_conc$x,
  mean = round(within_conc$y[, "mean"], 2),
  sd = round(within_conc$y[, "sd"], 3),
  cv = round(within_conc$y[, "sd"] / within_conc$y[, "mean"], 3)
)
```

The coefficient of variation is not constant (it is 0.02 to 0.04 at concentrations of 0.1 to 3, and 0.12 to 0.13 at 30 and 100), which shows that a `Gamma` on its own will not describe these data. We fit the model with and without a variance function and compare them.

```{r disp-fits, results = "hide"}
# seed is passed through to brm() so that both fits are exactly reproducible.
# set.seed() alone is not sufficient: it governs the initial values bayesnec
# generates, but not the seed Stan's sampler uses, so repeated runs differ.
disp_const <- bnec(y ~ crf(log(x), model = "ecx4param"),
                   data = disp_dat, seed = 333)
disp_power <- bnec(y ~ crf(log(x), model = "ecx4param") + disp("power"),
                   data = disp_dat, seed = 333)
```

```{r disp-compare}
brms::loo_compare(brms::loo(pull_brmsfit(disp_const)),
                  brms::loo(pull_brmsfit(disp_power)))
```

The model with the variance function has the higher expected log predictive density (the constant-dispersion model has an `elpd_diff` of −25.7, with a standard error of 5.4).

The estimated exponent should be interpreted through the family. `disp("power")` models the dispersion parameter, which for a `Gamma` is `shape`, and the implied standard deviation is proportional to $\mu^{1 - c_1/2}$ (see the table in `?bayesnecformula`). The value used to simulate the data, $\mu^{0.4}$, therefore corresponds to $c_1 = 1.2$:

```{r disp-slope}
brms::fixef(pull_brmsfit(disp_power))[c("c0_Intercept", "c1_Intercept"), ]
```

The estimate of 1.30 (95% interval 0.94 to 1.68) includes this value. A value of zero would correspond to the constant-dispersion model (i.e. the constant coefficient of variation a `Gamma` already implies). The default prior bounds `c1` to the range 0 to 2 for a `Gamma` response, from a constant coefficient of variation to a constant variance, so a posterior concentrated at 0 would show that the data do not support the variance function, whereas one well inside the range reflects the data and not the bound.

Finally, for the third check (lack of fit in the mean), we calculate the departure of the fitted curve from the observed mean at each concentration, in units of the within-concentration replicate standard deviation. Values of a few standard deviations or more would indicate a problem with the mean model that a variance function would conceal and not resolve.

```{r disp-lof}
disp_fitted <- colMeans(posterior_epred(disp_power))
lof <- aggregate(cbind(obs = disp_dat$y, fit = disp_fitted) ~ disp_dat$x, FUN = mean)
names(lof)[1] <- "x"
lof$replicate_sd <- tapply(disp_dat$y, disp_dat$x, sd)
lof$misfit_in_sds <- round(abs(lof$obs - lof$fit) / lof$replicate_sd, 2)
lof[, c("x", "replicate_sd", "misfit_in_sds")]
```

The departures are at most 0.21 replicate standard deviations at each concentration, so there is no evidence of lack of fit in the mean.

## Consequences for the intervals and estimates

The constant-dispersion and `disp("power")` models describe the same mean curve, so the effect of the variance function is on the intervals and not on the fitted response. We calculate two intervals from one set of posterior draws for each model: the *credible* interval on the fitted mean, and the *prediction* interval within which a new observation would be expected to fall. Only the prediction interval includes the fitted dispersion directly.

```{r disp-bands-code}
disp_grid <- data.frame(
  x = exp(seq(log(min(disp_dat$x)), log(max(disp_dat$x)), length.out = 100))
)

disp_bands <- function(fit, label) {
  bfit <- pull_brmsfit(fit)
  quants <- function(mat) {
    data.frame(x = disp_grid$x,
               lo = apply(mat, 2, quantile, 0.025),
               md = apply(mat, 2, median),
               hi = apply(mat, 2, quantile, 0.975))
  }
  rbind(
    cbind(quants(brms::posterior_epred(bfit, newdata = disp_grid)),
          model = label, interval = "credible interval (fitted mean)"),
    cbind(quants(brms::posterior_predict(bfit, newdata = disp_grid)),
          model = label, interval = "prediction interval (observations)")
  )
}

bands <- rbind(disp_bands(disp_const, "constant dispersion"),
               disp_bands(disp_power, "disp(\"power\")"))
```

```{r exmp1-disp-bands, fig.width = 7, fig.asp = 0.48, out.width = "100%"}
ggplot(bands, aes(x = x)) +
  geom_ribbon(aes(ymin = lo, ymax = hi, fill = model), alpha = 0.3) +
  geom_line(aes(y = md, colour = model)) +
  geom_point(data = disp_dat, aes(x = x, y = y), size = 0.7, alpha = 0.5,
             inherit.aes = FALSE) +
  facet_wrap(~interval) +
  scale_x_log10() +
  scale_y_log10() +
  labs(x = "Concentration", y = "Response", fill = NULL, colour = NULL) +
  theme_classic() +
  theme(legend.position = "top")
```

In the figure above, the median curves of the constant-dispersion and `disp("power")` fits are indistinguishable. At the low concentrations, where the replicates agree to within about 4 per cent of their mean, the constant-dispersion prediction interval is about twice as wide as the interval from the variance function. At the high concentrations, where the coefficient of variation has increased to about 0.12, it is narrower than the data warrant. This is the uneven error described at the start of this section, and it is the reason that the consequences of the variance function are for the toxicity estimates and not for the fitted curve. These consequences are also uneven across the toxicity estimates.

```{r disp-tox}
tox <- function(fit, label) {
  vals <- rbind(ec10 = ecx(fit, ecx_val = 10, xform = exp),
                ec50 = ecx(fit, ecx_val = 50, xform = exp),
                nsec = nsec(fit, xform = exp))
  data.frame(model = label, estimate = rownames(vals),
             median = round(vals[, 1], 2),
             lower = round(vals[, 2], 2), upper = round(vals[, 3], 2),
             width = round(vals[, 3] - vals[, 2], 2), row.names = NULL)
}
rbind(tox(disp_const, "constant dispersion"), tox(disp_power, "disp(\"power\")"))
```

The intervals of all three estimates are narrower under the variance function, but only the `nsec` median changes appreciably, declining by about two fifths, whereas the `ecx` medians change by a few per cent. The explanation is what each estimate is referenced against, which follows from the reweighting performed by the variance function. Under constant dispersion, all observations are given one coefficient of variation. Under `disp("power")`, the tightly grouped control replicates are given more weight and the variable high-concentration replicates less, and this is visible in the precision of the curve parameters themselves:

```{r disp-params}
# Only the curve parameters are shared: the disp fit carries c0 and c1 as well.
curve_pars <- c("top_Intercept", "bot_Intercept", "beta_Intercept", "ec50_Intercept")
round(cbind(
  constant = brms::fixef(pull_brmsfit(disp_const))[curve_pars, "Est.Error"],
  power = brms::fixef(pull_brmsfit(disp_power))[curve_pars, "Est.Error"]
), 4)
```

Under `disp("power")`, `top` (which is determined by the control end of the curve) is estimated about twice as precisely, `beta` and `ec50` somewhat more precisely, and `bot` (which is determined by the high-concentration end) less precisely. `ecx` depends on the curve as a whole (with `type = "absolute"`, the default, on where the curve declines to a given percentage of its own fitted maximum), so its estimate reflects the better-determined shape. In contrast, `nsec` is referenced against the control, as the concentration at which the curve drops below a lower quantile of the *control* posterior. The constant-dispersion fit overstates the spread of the control replicates, which places that quantile lower and the `nsec` at a higher concentration.

When choosing an endpoint, note that an `nsec` reported from a constant-dispersion fit depends on an assumption about control variability that the fit has not checked. In this example, that assumption gave the higher `nsec`, which is the less protective direction.

# References
